    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "Something like this (obviously trivial use case):\r\n```py\r\n@generated_git_with_cuda(inline=\"always\")\r\ndef grid_position_like(index):\r\n      if 'int16' in str(index):  # NB having trouble writing canonical checks for types\r\n          def grid_position_like(index):\r\n               i_x, i_y = cuda.grid(2)\r\n               return (np.int16(i_x), np.int16(i_y))\r\n      elif 'int32' in str(index):\r\n          def grid_position_like(index):\r\n               i_x, i_y = cuda.grid(2)\r\n               return (np.int32(i_x), np.int32(i_y))\r\n      elif 'int64' in str(index):\r\n          def grid_position_like(index):\r\n               i_x, i_y = cuda.grid(2)\r\n               return (np.int64(i_x), np.int64(i_y))\r\n      else:\r\n          raise TypeError(\"Grid position not supported for type\")\r\n      return grid_position_like\r\n```\r\nOf course, for this example, easy enough to put the `cuda.grid()` outside. I've got some utilities that keep track of state while doing prefix scans that might benefit from being flexible as wholes.\r\n\r\n**UPDATE** -- perhaps unrealistic to put `cuda.grid` in the generated_jit function but not in the returned functions. Changed to put inside generated snippets.",
